SILICONFOREST LICENSING NOTICE

Warning: this tree is a licensing mess.

The code is not organized by licence. Different licences are mixed across
directories. There are no reliable per-file headers or SPDX tags. A directory
name, a NOTICE file, a credits list, or an SPDX line on a neighbouring file
does not tell you the licence of the file in front of you. Anyone reusing any
of this code has to check that file's own provenance: what the file itself
says, if it says anything, and where the text came from.


This repository is not licensed as a whole under the MIT License.

This file is a notice. It is not a grant of rights in the tree, and it does
not change the licence of any file. The plain MIT text that used to be the
whole of this file applied, on this repository's own statement, only to original
SiliconForest code. Putting that text here made the repository look as if one
MIT grant covered everything in it. It does not.

Each file or component is governed by the licence of the code it derives from,
on that code's own terms. Where it is unclear what a file derives from, or
which licence that source used, this notice does not decide. Do not guess.


Crediting a source does not satisfy its licence.

README.md ("Credits and lineage") and NOTICE name upstream projects. That is
attribution. It is not compliance. GPL-2.0 and APSL-2.0 code, and the other
licences in this tree, carry their own terms: the licence text, copyright
notices, source-availability conditions, and the APSL's modification notices.
Because the code is mixed together without per-file separation, a credits list
cannot tell you which terms apply to which file.


Licences named in this tree
---------------------------

Full texts that were copied into the repository are in LICENSES/. LICENSES/README.md
says where each copy was taken from. Those files are the texts. They are not a
map of which source file is under which text.

Present as texts in LICENSES/, and named by NOTICE or by LICENSES/README.md as
used by some code here:

  MIT                  LICENSES/MIT.txt
  APSL-2.0             LICENSES/APSL-2.0.txt
  GPL-2.0-only         LICENSES/GPL-2.0-only.txt
                       (also the text of GPL-2.0-or-later, as that README notes)
  GPL-3.0-only         LICENSES/GPL-3.0-only.txt
  LGPL-2.1-or-later    LICENSES/LGPL-2.1-or-later.txt
  Apache-2.0           LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
  MPL-2.0              LICENSES/MPL-2.0.txt
  BSD-2-Clause         LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause.txt
  ISC                  LICENSES/ISC.txt
  OFL-1.1              LICENSES/OFL-1.1.txt
  Linux-syscall-note   LICENSES/Linux-syscall-note.txt
  0BSD                 LICENSES/third-party/Indium-LICENSE-0BSD.txt

LICENSES/third-party/ also holds licence files copied with their copyright
lines: PureDarwin's own PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt (BSD-2-Clause, "Copyright (c)
2007-Present The PureDarwin Project"), Finch (MIT and Apache-2.0, "Copyright
(c) 2026 The Finch Project contributors"), and m1n1 (MIT, "Copyright The Asahi
Linux Contributors"). The PureDarwin file is the project's licence file. It
is not the licence of every file in PureDarwin. See "The PureDarwin base is
itself mixed" below.

Named by LICENSES/README.md as used by a file here, with no template text in
LICENSES/:

  BSD-3-Clause         one header is (GPL-2.0+ WITH Linux-syscall-note) OR BSD-3-Clause
  Swift-exception      Apache-2.0 WITH Swift-exception, one file

Apple's "Binary Driver Software License Agreement" is named in NOTICE. It is
not an open-source licence. Its text is not in this repository. NOTICE says
which PureDarwin components it covers has not been established here. Nothing
in this repository is offered under it.

Other licences are named in NOTICE and marked there as not verified, because
no licence file was read for them in this repository (Mesa, parts of
drm-kmod and FreeBSD, Firefox as run here, neofetch, several fonts, and
components reached only through the build). Those statements are leads, not
assignments.


What "MIT for original SiliconForest code" does and does not mean
-----------------------------------------------------------------

LICENSING.md says the MIT License covers files written for
SiliconForest that were not ported, adopted, or copied. The copyright line
that stood in this file was:

  Copyright (c) 2026 Chris Kearney and the SiliconForest contributors

That line is the notice this repository attached to that claim. It is not a copyright
notice for the rest of the tree. The MIT terms, where they actually apply to a
file, are the text in LICENSES/MIT.txt together with that file's own copyright
notice.

The claim is not established file by file:

  - AGENTS.md still says: "License selection for original code is pending;
    do not relicense upstream code."
  - docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md item 6 says about 1,975 files carry
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT with no copyright line, and that those tags
    were not confirmed as this project's to apply. The same item says many
    GPL, ISC, BSD, and MPL files have no copyright or author line, and that
    ten directories with GPL-derived code have no NOTICE file.
  - Many PROVENANCE notes under integrations/siliconforest/ still say
    original-code licence selection is pending.
  - A large part of the tree has no SPDX line at all. core/, contracts/,
    probe/, userspace/, specs/, most of tools/ and tests/, and many
    integrations/siliconforest/ leaves are in that set. Their licence is
    unclear from the file.

Where a file is in fact original SiliconForest code and was in fact placed
under MIT, MIT applies to that file. Where you cannot show both, do not treat
the file as MIT. An SPDX MIT tag with no copyright line is not that showing.


Other facts the tree itself records
-----------------------------------

NOTICE is a provenance record. Its own header says it is not a legal review,
and many of its entries are marked "not verified". Per-directory NOTICE files
are more specific where they exist, and NOTICE says ten directories with
GPL-derived code do not have one: m1-rust-kext, m1-gpu-rtkit, m1-dcp,
m1-dcp-dart, m1-lkpi-extras, m1-cpufreq, m1-thermal, m1-drm72, m1-native-boot,
m1-avd-capacity.

Code taken from Linux and Asahi Linux is recorded as GPL-2.0-only, or as a
dual licence where the file says so (GPL-2.0-only OR MIT, and similar). The
vendored Chonkstep copy's LICENSE file and its Cargo.toml
(`license = "GPL-3.0-only"`) are GPL-3.0-only; NOTICE also records MIT
licence files inside that copy (dock/bindings and examples). Patches to
Apple's open-source releases and the copies of IOHIDEventService.cpp under
m1-native-input/ carry the APSL-2.0 header. Finch is recorded as
MIT OR Apache-2.0. OpenBSD bwfm is recorded as ISC. m1n1 is recorded as MIT.
These are the statements in NOTICE and in the licence files. They are not a
substitute for opening the file. Directories mix these together.


The PureDarwin base is itself mixed
------------------------------------

SiliconForest's base tree is PureDarwin (NOTICE: branch upstream-xnu-12377 at
c713d8fb). That tree is not BSD-2-Clause as a whole.

PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt, copied to LICENSES/third-party/PureDarwin-LICENSE.txt,
is BSD-2-Clause for the PureDarwin Project's own licence file. A PureDarwin
maintainer wrote, on this repository's roadmap issue, that not all of PureDarwin
is BSD-2-Clause: it is a mix of several licences, including pure MPL-2.0,
MPL-2.0 OR MIT, and some tri-licensed files:

  https://github.com/chriskearney/siliconforest/issues/23#issuecomment-6984090313

This repository has not listed those tri-licences, and it does not assign
them to files from that sentence. NOTICE had already recorded a further case
from files' own notices: some newer PureDarwin files are MPL-2.0 OR
BSD-2-Clause. Apple's XNU and the other Darwin code PureDarwin builds are
APSL-2.0, or the other licence that file's own header names (NOTICE's Apple
section; LICENSES/APSL-2.0.txt, from upstream's APPLE_LICENSE.txt).

The licence of a PureDarwin file is the notice on that file in upstream
PureDarwin, not this paragraph and not PUREDARWIN_LICENSE.txt:

  https://github.com/PureDarwin/PureDarwin

docs/roadmap/issues/SF-0-index.md, and issue 23, which carries the
same text, still say "BSD-2-Clause for its own files". That line is too
small. The maintainer's comment and the per-file headers are the correction.


Unresolved: distribution of combined binaries
---------------------------------------------

This is an open question. It is not legal advice. This repository does not
answer it.

GPL-2.0-only kernel extensions ported from Asahi Linux are built as kexts
and prelinked into the same kernel collection as XNU. XNU in this tree is
Apple code under APSL-2.0. docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md item 4 records that
combination and leaves it open: on what terms a built collection or system
image may be distributed. The same maintainer comment asks how distribution
of kernel collections with GPL-2.0 and APSL-2.0 code is supposed to work.

The FSF's licence list describes the Apple Public Source License, version
2.0, as a free software licence that is incompatible with the GPL:

  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/license-list.html#apsl2

If that view applies here, distributing that kernel collection may not be
possible under both licences at once. Nothing in this tree chooses a way
through that. The checklist says that publishing the sources side by side,
each under its own licence, is common practice. It does not say that this
makes the combined binary distributable. No decision is recorded.

A second incompatibility, where the combination exists. The FSF's licence
list describes the Apache License, version 2.0, as incompatible with GPL
version 2:

  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/license-list.html#apache2

Finch is recorded as MIT OR Apache-2.0 (NOTICE, and issue 23). The
GPL-2.0-only code this tree identifies is the Asahi-derived kexts and the
other GPL-2.0-only ports (the AVE driver among them). Those are kernel
extensions. Finch is recorded as userspace frameworks. This notice does not
find a build that links a Finch object into a GPL-2.0-only kext, and it does
not record an election of Finch's MIT side or its Apache-2.0 side. The MIT
option and the Apache-2.0 option are different terms. If a binary takes Finch
code under Apache-2.0 and combines it with GPL-2.0-only code, that is the
incompatibility the FSF describes, and this repository has no answer for that
either. The files are not separated by licence, so a credits line is not
proof they were kept apart.

The checklist also notes that the GPL-3.0-only desktop links the APSL
libSystem. That combination is not answered here either.


Notes left in the tree
----------------------

The release checklist in this repository, which still lists the licensing work that was
not finished:

  docs/RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md

Item 4 is the GPL-2.0 / APSL kernel-collection question. Item 6 is per-file
headers. The checklist records that the owner later chose to make the
repository public with that work unfinished. Making the repository public
did not finish it.

The standing instruction in AGENTS.md is unchanged: "License selection for
original code is pending; do not relicense upstream code."

The README of 7 to 9 November 2026, kept as a record, said that no
project-wide distribution licence had been selected:

  docs/history/README-2026-11-09.md

How the texts and the provenance record fit together is in LICENSING.md.
What is still unresolved is in LICENSE_STATUS.md.
